<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan of England, Queen of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1165">1165</a>–1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. She married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily">William II of Sicily</a> in 1177 and, after his death, was freed by her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, whom she accompanied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. In 1196 she married Raymond VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agenais">Agenais</a> as her dowry, and was mother of Raymond VII. She died in childbirth in 1199.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-england-queen-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Joan of England, Queen of Sicily" width="254" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joan_of_England.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joan was born in October 1165 at the Château d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> in Anjou, the seventh child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. As an infant she travelled with her mother's household through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and much of her childhood was spent at Fontevraud Abbey alongside her younger brother John. Her education prepared her for a dynastic marriage. In 1174, after the Revolt of 1173–1174, her father removed her from Fontevraud and sent her to Salisbury and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, where her mother was under house arrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1176 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily">William II of Sicily</a> sent ambassadors to ask for her hand, seeking an alliance with Henry II, who was then one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s pre-eminent figures. Henry raised money for the journey and dowry by taxing his English subjects. Joan sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> in late August and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in late January. On 13 February 1177 she married William and was crowned Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-cathedral">Palermo Cathedral</a>. She had no surviving heir, though a rumoured son Bohemond, born in 1181 or 1182, is said to have died in infancy if he existed. William did not annul the marriage and named his aunt Constance as heir.</p><p>After William died in November 1189, his cousin Tancred seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, took the lands William had given Joan and put her under house arrest at Zisa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in retaliation for her backing of Constance. Richard I arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1190 on his way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and demanded her return and her dowry. He seized a monastery and the castle of La Bagnara and subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, and Tancred finally agreed. In 1191 Joan sailed with Richard's bride, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-navarre">Berengaria of Navarre</a>, and the fleet was scattered by a storm. Richard rescued them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and sent them on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. He proposed marrying her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">Al-Adil</a>, but high-ranking priests opposed the plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In October 1196 Joan married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI, Count of Toulouse</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, as his fourth wife. She was the mother of Raymond VII, born in July 1197, and of a daughter, Joan, born in 1198. The Chronicle of Guillaume de Puylaurens says she took arms against the lords of Saint-Felix and besieged their castle at Les Cassés, then hurried to her brother Richard, only to find he had died. She was admitted to Fontevrault Abbey and veiled a nun on her deathbed, and died in childbirth in 1199, aged thirty-three. Her son lived long enough to be baptised Richard. She was buried at Rouen and later re-interred at Fontevrault.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joan of England, Queen of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dynastic marriages tied the house to the great royal families: Raymond V married Constance, sister of Louis VII of France, and Raymond VI married <b>Joan of England</b>, sister of Richard I, who brought the Agenais as her dowry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château d'Angers, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.56&amp;year=1187" aria-label="Château d'Angers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4537 499 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joan of England, Queen of Sicily"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4987 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4987 769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.56&amp;year=1187">What was here in 1187? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
